And then I would probably want
to run commands on those boxes.

00:10:55.660 --> 00:10:59.560
So I can do that-- gcloud
compute ssh virtual machine

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:01.690
name, and the command
I want to run locally--

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:04.190
or remotely.

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:05.950
And then finally,
I have files that I

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:07.390
want to get onto those VMs.

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:10.210
I can do that with
gcloud compute scp.

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:12.400
So between these
three, I can completely

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:16.330
automate the creation
and configuration

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:18.960
of a machine repeatably.

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:20.030
And I do a lot of this.

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:21.613
I do a lot of scripting
of this stuff,

00:11:21.613 --> 00:11:25.080
because I don't want to do it by
hand, because I make mistakes.

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:27.900
So we talked about virtual
machines and disk images.

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:30.360
So yup, you can start
with ours, like I did.

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.390
You create and put all the
software on it, make a disk,

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:34.874
and then save it as an image.

00:11:34.874 --> 00:11:36.540
But you can also make
them from scratch.

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:39.660
You could use VirtualBox
to make images from scratch

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:41.430
and upload them to us.

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:42.840
Networking, I talked about--

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:43.756
little bit about that.

00:11:43.756 --> 00:11:44.640
You get external IPs.

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:46.431
But one of t the things
I didn't talk about

00:11:46.431 --> 00:11:49.380
is that you can always
get an IP address,

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:51.870
and it'll always be
ephemeral, meaning,

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:54.802
if the machine goes down,
when it comes back up,

00:11:54.802 --> 00:11:56.010
it'll get another IP address.

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:58.170
But any of those
IP addresses, you

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:00.960
can grab and say,
make static and keep.

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:02.910
And so if you start
fooling around with a box,

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:05.190
and you set it to ephemeral,
and you're now worried,

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:07.731
because stuff is now depending
on the IP address of this box,

00:12:07.731 --> 00:12:09.101
you can grab it, hold onto it.

00:12:09.101 --> 00:12:10.350
We don't charge you for those.

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:12.450
The only time we charge you--
and this is something that

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:13.950
members of my team
just learned--

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:16.300
is, we only charge you for
it if you don't use it.

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:18.780
So if you have an IP address,
and it's in use statically,

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:19.905
we don't charge you for it.

00:12:19.905 --> 00:12:22.974
It's when you park on it that we
have a little, nominal charge.

00:12:22.974 --> 00:12:24.390
Just a couple of
cents-- it's just

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:26.940
enough to kind of
disincentivize you

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:30.990
from holding onto IP
addresses for a long time,

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:34.005
or holding onto a lot of IP
addresses for a long time.

00:12:34.005 --> 00:12:36.130
Showed off the firewall,
that you can create rules.

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:38.832
I just did the HTTP rule,
which we've kind of automated.

00:12:38.832 --> 00:12:40.290
But you can create
arbitrary rules,

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:41.706
and they get applied
the same way.

00:12:41.706 --> 00:12:44.940
They get this tag that gets
applied to your machine.

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:48.660
And that's what will determine
whether or not your firewall is

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:50.540
open on the machine.

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:52.400
One thing I didn't
show off is something

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:54.500
called Cloud Launcher.

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:57.860
Our VMs, our images, start
at the software layer, the OS

00:12:57.860 --> 00:12:58.490
layer.

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:00.290
But if you want, I
don't know, like,

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:03.200
Cassandra, or WordPress,
or Drupal, or something

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:05.150
like that on your
machine, you can

00:13:05.150 --> 00:13:09.830
use one of these and shortcut
having to do the whole build.

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:13.640
Now, it's important to know
that we don't manage it

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:14.900
once you build it.

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:16.439
We just help you build it.

00:13:16.439 --> 00:13:18.980
All right, I'm going to take a
slight detour and talk about--

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:20.840
I talk about the Cloud
Shell for a second.

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:21.840
That's really important.

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:23.300
Like I said, it's a little VM.

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:25.460
And you have a home directory
that follows you, which is--

00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:27.830
like, you shove scripts in
there that you use all the time,

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:30.290
and they'll be there the next
time you go to Cloud Shell.

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:33.170
And it basically means,
I never have to--

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:34.760
while I love having
my laptop, I never

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:37.010
actually have to have
it somewhere when

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:40.059
I might need to log in
to Google Cloud Platform.

00:13:40.059 --> 00:13:42.100
All right, we're back from
that little diversion.

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:43.810
So now, let's talk
about storage, right?

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:46.360
Because that's the crux
of what this app does.

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:48.460
It stores files,
and it stores data.

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:52.010
I'm going to start with the
files part of it, the images.

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:55.490
So for images, we call that
blob data, because I don't know,

00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:58.030
it sounds gross, I guess.

00:13:58.030 --> 00:13:59.810
Cloud Storage is
the tool for that.

00:13:59.810 --> 00:14:02.020
So it's for files, for
videos, for pictures,

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:05.734
for unstructured documents.

00:14:05.734 --> 00:14:07.900
Now, there are four types,
and I'll talk about them,

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:09.441
but they all have
the same interface.

00:14:09.441 --> 00:14:12.490
And the metaphors you've seen
with other cloud providers,

00:14:12.490 --> 00:14:16.300
the idea of a bucket and
objects in that bucket, that's

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:17.390
what cloud files--

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:18.840
that's how Cloud Storage works.

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:20.630
So we have the four types.

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:24.340
We've got multi-regional,
regional, nearline and

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:25.540
coldline.

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:28.390
and they all have different
use cases and different costs

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:29.830
associated with it.

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:32.620
Long story short, multi-regional
is for global data.

00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:34.950
It's for you pushing the
data as close to the people

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:36.200
that are going to be using it.

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:37.866
So it's for people
outside your company.

00:14:37.866 --> 00:14:39.310
It's for web presence.

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:40.355
It's for web apps.

00:14:40.355 --> 00:14:41.230
It's for mobile apps.

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:43.780
It's for streaming video.

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:46.310
Regional, in
contrast, is, you want

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:48.310
to keep the data close
to where it's being used,

00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:49.143
just like in global.

00:14:49.143 --> 00:14:51.340
But for this, it's being
used for internal purposes.

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:54.190
It's for running data
jobs and doing big data

00:14:54.190 --> 00:14:56.340
analysis on large data sets.

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:58.090
You want that data
near the computers that

00:14:58.090 --> 00:15:00.131
are going to be doing
that, but don't necessarily

00:15:00.131 --> 00:15:01.390
have to be near the public.

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:03.130
Now, another thing
to note here is, just

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:04.546
because it's
regional doesn't mean

00:15:04.546 --> 00:15:07.420
it's only accessible
from the region it's in.

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:09.814
It just means that it is
fastest in that region.

00:15:09.814 --> 00:15:11.230
And you could still
pull this data

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:12.710
from other places
around the world.

00:15:12.710 --> 00:15:17.030
So you're not restricted of
where you can use data from.

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:19.340
Nearline and coldline are
long-term storage options.

00:15:19.340 --> 00:15:21.650
Therefore, in the
case of nearline,

00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:23.780
it's for backups and
long tail media--

00:15:23.780 --> 00:15:26.930
stuff that's going to be used
regularly, but infrequently.

00:15:26.930 --> 00:15:29.480
And for coldline, it's things
like compliance and disaster

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:30.540
recovery.

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:32.630
So if you're pulling
down data from coldline,

00:15:32.630 --> 00:15:35.390
you're probably also loading
your resume in an editor,

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:38.780
just in case, right?

00:15:38.780 --> 00:15:41.180
So that's sort of the
distinction we use for these.

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:44.360
Now, as we go to
the right, the price

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:46.910
goes down, because it's
less accessible and less

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:47.900
easy to get to.

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:51.110
The time to first byte for
global or multi-regional versus

00:15:51.110 --> 00:15:52.130
coldline--

00:15:52.130 --> 00:15:56.060
coldline is a little bit
slower, time to first bite.

00:15:56.060 --> 00:15:58.650
And so accordingly,
the costs go down.

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:02.390
But for nearline and coldline,
when you retrieve the data,

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:04.080
you get charged for it.

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:07.810
And in this case, it's
one gigabyte per--

00:16:07.810 --> 00:16:11.390
I'm sorry, one-- all those
prices were gigabyte per month.

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:12.600
This is per gigabyte.

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:14.660
So when you pull down
data out of nearline,

00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:17.360
it's going to cost
you $0.01 gigabyte.

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:21.800
And out of coldline,
$0.05 per gigabyte.

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:24.020
The idea here is, we want
to encourage you, like,

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:27.432
to store stuff, and hope
you never need to use it.

00:16:27.432 --> 00:16:29.390
And that's why the pricing
is set up that way--

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:31.105
to encourage that sort of use.

00:16:31.105 --> 00:16:33.230
Now, I'm going to do a
quick demo of Cloud Storage.

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:36.100
So I'm going to switch
back to my laptop here.

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:38.810
I'm going to show how easy
it is to set one of these up.

00:16:38.810 --> 00:16:41.060
I'm going to go ahead, and
like I said, it's a bucket.

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:42.393
So I'm going to create a bucket.

00:16:42.393 --> 00:16:47.270
I'm going to call it
tagger-102, because the names

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:48.842
have to be unique.

00:16:48.842 --> 00:16:50.550
I'm going to just go
ahead and create it.

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:51.890
It's multi-regional.

00:16:51.890 --> 00:16:54.677
So I'm now creating a
multi-regional bucket.

00:16:54.677 --> 00:16:55.760
It's already ready for me.

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:57.440
I'm going to upload the files.

00:16:57.440 --> 00:17:00.340
I'm going to take my files
from here that I already had.

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:01.442
I'm going to upload them.

00:17:01.442 --> 00:17:02.900
Let's see how our
network is doing.

00:17:06.170 --> 00:17:12.020
OK, Wi-Fi network is--

00:17:12.020 --> 00:17:13.530
there we go, all right.

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:16.760
So you'll see, I have a bunch
of images here, now, loaded in.

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:19.170
Now, I can't get
to any of these.

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:21.270
I mean, Google Cloud can
see these and share them

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:22.440
among Google Cloud apps.

00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:24.869
But the public can't see them.

00:17:24.869 --> 00:17:26.609
The way I enable them
to the public is,

00:17:26.609 --> 00:17:28.800
I can just click this
little thing here and say,

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:30.930
I want to share publicly.

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:32.724
And when I do that,
I get a public link.

00:17:32.724 --> 00:17:34.140
So even though I
just uploaded it,

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:37.110
it's now globally available
and able to pull down.

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:39.792
If I were to URL
hack and change it

00:17:39.792 --> 00:17:42.000
to-- one of the other images
was that kid in the Ewok

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:45.310
costume, which is
ewok.jpg, I hit ewok.jpg,

00:17:45.310 --> 00:17:46.492
and it's not there.

00:17:46.492 --> 00:17:48.450
So you can be very granular
with what you share

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:50.160
and what you don't share.

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:52.890
So I'm now going to
switch back to presenting.

00:17:52.890 --> 00:17:53.390
Oh, wait.

00:17:53.390 --> 00:17:54.430
I'm not going to switch back.

00:17:54.430 --> 00:17:55.513
It'll switch back on this.

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:01.390
And let's talk about a
little bit of what I did.

00:18:01.390 --> 00:18:02.560
I wanted to upload files.

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:04.750
And you could do that with the
gsutil command on the command

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:04.990
line.

00:18:04.990 --> 00:18:06.489
That's how I do
most of this, right?

00:18:06.489 --> 00:18:10.060
Because I don't want to manually
put all my files through a web

00:18:10.060 --> 00:18:11.750
interface.

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:14.200
I can also share
publicly using gsutil.

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:15.310
I can set up the settings.

00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:16.910
You can do them per file.

00:18:16.910 --> 00:18:18.250
You can also do them per bucket.

00:18:18.250 --> 00:18:19.750
That's how I tend to do it.

00:18:19.750 --> 00:18:21.844
I share the bucket,
and then I try

00:18:21.844 --> 00:18:24.010
not to have shared and
non-shared stuff in the same,

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:25.240
even though you can.

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:27.850
Just saying, for me, I don't
want to accidentally share

00:18:27.850 --> 00:18:28.370
something.

00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:31.090
So I usually try to
make that distinction

00:18:31.090 --> 00:18:33.590
at the bucket level.

00:18:33.590 --> 00:18:38.770
Now, my app was writing to
file storage on the server.

00:18:38.770 --> 00:18:41.350
I now need it to
write a Cloud Storage.

00:18:41.350 --> 00:18:43.600
So for that, I want to
use our client libraries.

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:44.830
Now, our client
libraries, you'll see,

00:18:44.830 --> 00:18:46.204
there's a whole
list of languages

00:18:46.204 --> 00:18:47.260
they're available for.

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:50.960
If your favorite language is
not here, one, I apologize.

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:54.550
Two, we have a REST API, and
it's pretty well documented.

00:18:54.550 --> 00:19:00.400
So you can do this stuff without
needing the client library.

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:01.477
But this app was PHP.

00:19:01.477 --> 00:19:02.560
So I don't have a problem.

00:19:02.560 --> 00:19:03.684
So let's walk through this.

00:19:03.684 --> 00:19:05.800
Please, don't judge me for PHP.

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:08.290
I figured it was very readable.

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:11.160
move_uploaded_file,
source, destination.

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:13.420
So something has been
uploaded to the server.

00:19:13.420 --> 00:19:15.460
We move it to a file location.

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:18.370
To do the same thing, but
point it to Cloud Storage,

00:19:18.370 --> 00:19:20.770
I have to pull in
the storage library

00:19:20.770 --> 00:19:24.100
and then create a storage
client and a bucket and upload

00:19:24.100 --> 00:19:24.850
it to that bucket.

00:19:24.850 --> 00:19:27.700
It's a little bit more code,
but not tremendously more.

00:19:27.700 --> 00:19:30.010
And dropping it
in was relatively

00:19:30.010 --> 00:19:32.600
simple and painless to do.

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:34.010
So that is client library.

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:35.390
So now, here's where we are.

00:19:35.390 --> 00:19:37.019
We've carved off images.

00:19:37.019 --> 00:19:39.060
We now have our application
code and our database

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:39.685
still together.

00:19:39.685 --> 00:19:40.890
We need to fix that.

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:42.530
So let's talk about databases.

00:19:42.530 --> 00:19:45.140
With that, we'll go to
Application Storage,

00:19:45.140 --> 00:19:46.850
and we get the SQL versus NoSQL.

00:19:46.850 --> 00:19:49.970
And that is not anything
that I want to wade into.

00:19:49.970 --> 00:19:53.150
I'm sure we all have beliefs
about SQL versus NoSQL.

00:19:53.150 --> 00:19:54.050
It's OK.

00:19:54.050 --> 00:19:56.480
I'm not going to
question any of them.

00:19:56.480 --> 00:19:58.490
But I'll talk about what
we have for these two

00:19:58.490 --> 00:20:00.570
places, or these two types.

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:04.280
So we have Cloud SQL, which
is a traditional SQL Server.

00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:06.890
It's MySQL or Postgres.

00:20:06.890 --> 00:20:08.990
And it's what we call
vertically scalable.

00:20:08.990 --> 00:20:10.940
You can keep making
the machine it's

00:20:10.940 --> 00:20:12.260
running on bigger and bigger.

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:14.390
But at a certain point,
when you need to scale out,

00:20:14.390 --> 00:20:16.700
these don't scale
out perfectly well.

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:19.600
You can do replica sets,
and we make that easy to do.

00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:23.780
But it still just good,
old-fashioned vanilla MySQL

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:25.596
and Postgres under the covers.

00:20:25.596 --> 00:20:26.720
We just manage the backups.

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:28.345
And we make it easy
to set up replicas.

00:20:28.345 --> 00:20:31.150
And we take a lot of the pain
out of running these machines.

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:33.441
And we also have this other
thing called Cloud Spanner,

00:20:33.441 --> 00:20:34.130
which--

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:37.340
let me put it in context-- you
would never use on this app.

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:40.610
It's for gigantic,
worldwide companies

00:20:40.610 --> 00:20:45.270
that have very high rates
of queries per second.

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:47.780
It is not for this tagger app.

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:49.310
But that's also
available to you.

00:20:49.310 --> 00:20:50.350
It has a SQL interface.

00:20:50.350 --> 00:20:53.330
It's structured data, highly
available, strongly consistent.

00:20:53.330 --> 00:20:57.650
Our network makes that work,
where we can make those claims

00:20:57.650 --> 00:20:59.589
that it is both those things.

00:20:59.589 --> 00:21:00.880
And it's horizontally scalable.

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:04.550
But keep in mind, it is
definitely cost effective

00:21:04.550 --> 00:21:06.300
when you have the scale for it.

00:21:06.300 --> 00:21:10.110
At this small app,
it would be overkill.

00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:11.420
So now, let's talk about NoSQL.

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:13.640
NoSQL, we've got Cloud
Datastore and Firestore,

00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:15.170
I'll start with first.

00:21:15.170 --> 00:21:18.220
Anybody here use Firebase?

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:21.740
OK, so Cloud Firestore has a lot
of integration with Firebase.

00:21:21.740 --> 00:21:24.490
And there's a similar product
called Cloud Firestore for--

00:21:24.490 --> 00:21:26.741
I forget the branding of it.

00:21:26.741 --> 00:21:27.740
But it's the same thing.

00:21:27.740 --> 00:21:30.470
And basically, it allows
you to use Firebase's APIs

00:21:30.470 --> 00:21:33.550
to write to a gigantic
NoSQL store in the back end.

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:36.050
It's really great when you're
writing mobile clients and web

00:21:36.050 --> 00:21:37.966
clients that you don't
want a back end at all.

00:21:37.966 --> 00:21:39.890
You don't want application
structure at all.

00:21:39.890 --> 00:21:41.210
You just basically
want the front-end app

00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:42.830
to talk directly
to the data store.

00:21:42.830 --> 00:21:44.600
That's a Cloud Firestore is for.

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:46.250
Cloud Datastore
is more for, I've

00:21:46.250 --> 00:21:48.320
got an app that I've
written that I also

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:49.952
want to store stuff to.

00:21:49.952 --> 00:21:51.410
And that's running
on the back end.

00:21:51.410 --> 00:21:53.868
And that communication would
happen between Cloud Datastore

00:21:53.868 --> 00:21:56.120
and your app.

00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:59.810
And finally, Bigtable, you
don't see a lot in applications.

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:01.490
Very rarely, someone
will come out

00:22:01.490 --> 00:22:03.364
and use this for an
application, because it's

00:22:03.364 --> 00:22:04.360
got very low latency.

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:07.190
But where it really
excels is big data jobs.

00:22:07.190 --> 00:22:09.260
You put a whole bunch
of logs in there,

00:22:09.260 --> 00:22:11.809
and store them, and run
through stuff in that.

00:22:11.809 --> 00:22:12.850
So that's Cloud Bigtable.

00:22:12.850 --> 00:22:16.380
It's another one that's only
cost effective at scale.

00:22:16.380 --> 00:22:17.970
So which one should we use?

00:22:17.970 --> 00:22:20.060
Well, I kind of set
this up so that there

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:21.740
is an obvious winner.

00:22:21.740 --> 00:22:26.420
I'm not going to rewrite
all my logic from SQL NoSQL.

00:22:26.420 --> 00:22:28.730
So I'm not going to use
any of the NoSQL solutions.

00:22:28.730 --> 00:22:30.080
And Cloud Spanner is overkill.

00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:32.780
So I'm going to use Cloud SQL.

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:33.830
We go to the next one.

00:22:33.830 --> 00:22:35.788
And I'm going to use
MySQL instead of Postgres,

00:22:35.788 --> 00:22:37.816
because again, I don't
want to rewrite stuff.

00:22:37.816 --> 00:22:39.440
So I'm going to do
a quick demo of this

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:41.660
to show you what this
interface looks like.

00:22:41.660 --> 00:22:43.810
I've got SQL here.

00:22:43.810 --> 00:22:45.260
I already have a
database set up.

00:22:45.260 --> 00:22:47.510
But I'm going to
create a new one.

00:22:47.510 --> 00:22:51.140
And you'll see, I got some
options, MySQL versus Postgres.

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:52.912
And we actually
kind of help you--

00:22:52.912 --> 00:22:54.620
like, what are you
going to use this for?

00:22:54.620 --> 00:22:56.200
Are you going to use it for
development, for staging,

00:22:56.200 --> 00:22:56.825
for production?

00:22:56.825 --> 00:22:59.150
I'm going to use
staging, because

00:22:59.150 --> 00:23:02.210
among its many features,
it has this, which is

00:23:02.210 --> 00:23:04.650
automatic increase in storage.

00:23:04.650 --> 00:23:08.060
So when I set up my
disks on the back end,

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:10.080
it will automatically
make my disk bigger

00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:13.880
if I start to run afoul of
the size of the disk, up

00:23:13.880 --> 00:23:15.750
to 64 terabytes.

00:23:15.750 --> 00:23:19.210
So I'm going to go ahead
and configure one of these.

00:23:19.210 --> 00:23:20.117
I can choose version.

00:23:20.117 --> 00:23:21.200
I can choose machine type.

00:23:21.200 --> 00:23:22.970
So I can make the
machine bigger.

00:23:22.970 --> 00:23:25.610
They're based on our VM images.

00:23:25.610 --> 00:23:26.760
You can't customize them.

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:30.770
But they kind of fit with
the VM standard images.

00:23:30.770 --> 00:23:34.979
You can choose SSD
or standard disk.

00:23:34.979 --> 00:23:36.770
But one of the cool
things you need to know

00:23:36.770 --> 00:23:39.620
is that if you need higher
read-write performance

00:23:39.620 --> 00:23:42.260
to your disks, you need
to get bigger drives.

00:23:42.260 --> 00:23:44.390
And you'll see, when I
change this number over here

00:23:44.390 --> 00:23:46.680
from 20 to 200,
all of a sudden, I

00:23:46.680 --> 00:23:49.640
get more IOPS and
disk throughput.

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:50.550
So keep that in mind.

00:23:50.550 --> 00:23:52.008
You might need to
make bigger disks

00:23:52.008 --> 00:23:53.906
to get better performance.

00:23:53.906 --> 00:23:55.280
OK, so I think
that's what I want

00:23:55.280 --> 00:23:57.920
to show off about Cloud SQL.

00:23:57.920 --> 00:24:00.130
So I'm going to switch
back to present mode.

00:24:04.792 --> 00:24:06.500
I'm going to switch
back to present mode.

00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:08.670
There you go.

00:24:08.670 --> 00:24:10.340
Oh, I'm pressing
the wrong button.

00:24:10.340 --> 00:24:11.390
I'm all over the place.

00:24:11.390 --> 00:24:13.780
All right, so how do I get
my stuff into Cloud SQL?

00:24:13.780 --> 00:24:14.930
This is relatively simple.

00:24:14.930 --> 00:24:15.530
I take it.

00:24:15.530 --> 00:24:17.480
I upload it to Cloud Storage.

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:19.280
And then, from Cloud
Storage, I ingest it

00:24:19.280 --> 00:24:22.490
into Cloud SQL, which brings
up something kind of important,

00:24:22.490 --> 00:24:25.400
which is, this workflow,
move it to Cloud Storage

00:24:25.400 --> 00:24:28.502
and then pull it somewhere
else, is actually pretty common.

00:24:28.502 --> 00:24:29.960
You have a whole
bunch of log data.

00:24:29.960 --> 00:24:32.385
You put it in Cloud Storage,
ingest it into BigQuery.

00:24:32.385 --> 00:24:34.760
You have images that you want
to analyze with our machine

00:24:34.760 --> 00:24:36.560
learning APIs, put
it into Cloud Storage

00:24:36.560 --> 00:24:38.657
and have them analyze it.

00:24:38.657 --> 00:24:40.490
And you'll see two tools
here in the center.

00:24:40.490 --> 00:24:42.701
Cloud Storage is one of
those that acts as a hub.

00:24:42.701 --> 00:24:44.700
The other one is something
called Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:24:44.700 --> 00:24:46.325
And there's actually
a really cool demo

00:24:46.325 --> 00:24:50.080
of Pub/Sub in the Cloud Dome,
in case you're interested.

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:53.420
But what Pub/Sub does
is, it's a message bus.

00:24:53.420 --> 00:24:54.974
You say, I'm a
publisher, and there's

00:24:54.974 --> 00:24:57.140
a whole bunch of people who
subscribe to the channel

00:24:57.140 --> 00:24:58.250
that you publish to.

00:24:58.250 --> 00:25:00.590
You send a message, a
structured piece of data,

00:25:00.590 --> 00:25:02.000
that does something.

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:04.550
And it gets navigated--
it gets communicated out

00:25:04.550 --> 00:25:07.250
to all the people that
are-- all the subscribers

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:08.930
of that service.

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:10.790
It's very high throughput.

00:25:10.790 --> 00:25:12.770
So basically, it
allows you to basically

00:25:12.770 --> 00:25:15.920
offload stuff to other pieces
of the cloud when you need to,

00:25:15.920 --> 00:25:18.710
and do it with a queuing system
that's really, really robust

00:25:18.710 --> 00:25:21.000
and fast.

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:24.140
OK, we'll segue back and talk
about some common problems

00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:28.010
people have when they start
running database servers on us.

00:25:28.010 --> 00:25:29.720
So one problem,
and I filed a bug,

00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:32.120
but I feel like I should
communicate it to you,

00:25:32.120 --> 00:25:36.740
is that when you run gcloud
sql to ingest the SQL,

00:25:36.740 --> 00:25:37.700
you might get an error.

00:25:37.700 --> 00:25:40.580
And the error is going to look
like this, INTERNAL_ERROR.

00:25:40.580 --> 00:25:42.110
And then if you
raise the verbosity

00:25:42.110 --> 00:25:44.480
of the gcloud command,
you get that it's

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:47.000
a SQL error, which is
helpful, but not really,

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:49.550
because we kind
of knew, it's SQL.

00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:53.090
So what you do is,
in the SQL interface,

00:25:53.090 --> 00:25:55.340
there's this tab
called Operations.

00:25:55.340 --> 00:25:57.590
And in Operations, we can
see that there was an error.

00:25:57.590 --> 00:25:59.089
We can actually see
the line number.

00:25:59.089 --> 00:26:01.700
We see all of the SQL data that
we need to troubleshoot this.

00:26:01.700 --> 00:26:03.440
For this particular
error, it was

00:26:03.440 --> 00:26:06.466
that I had done a MySQL
dump, like you normally do.

00:26:06.466 --> 00:26:08.090
But I had some super
permissions, which

00:26:08.090 --> 00:26:09.680
you can't have on Cloud SQL.

00:26:09.680 --> 00:26:11.130
And so it balked at that.

00:26:11.130 --> 00:26:13.760
So just a couple things that, if
you're switching over to this,

00:26:13.760 --> 00:26:16.490
you might want to know
off the top of your head

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:21.080
before you headdesk trying
to track down what this is.

00:26:21.080 --> 00:26:23.420
Now, what happens
if we, Google Cloud,

00:26:23.420 --> 00:26:26.960
don't offer something
that you need?

00:26:26.960 --> 00:26:28.760
Like, we have
MySQL, and Postgres,

00:26:28.760 --> 00:26:30.620
and our NoSQL
solutions, but you want

00:26:30.620 --> 00:26:35.270
Cassandra, or Mongo, or one of
the other storage solutions.

00:26:35.270 --> 00:26:36.420
Well, you do two options.

00:26:36.420 --> 00:26:38.182
One, you can build it
yourself from hand--

00:26:38.182 --> 00:26:39.515
build it yourself from scratch--

00:26:42.080 --> 00:26:43.460
mixing metaphors.

00:26:43.460 --> 00:26:46.520
Or you could use Cloud Launcher
to build one of our existing

00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:48.650
solutions, like
Cassandra, Redis, Mongo,

00:26:48.650 --> 00:26:50.660
CouchDB and a whole bunch more.

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:53.960
In each of these cases, it is
important to note that we don't

00:26:53.960 --> 00:26:55.430
maintain the software for you.

00:26:55.430 --> 00:26:57.620
We maintain the OS.

00:26:57.620 --> 00:27:00.189
And so in all the cases
with Compute Engine,

00:27:00.189 --> 00:27:01.730
I didn't mention
this earlier, but we

00:27:01.730 --> 00:27:03.740
do something called
live migration.

00:27:03.740 --> 00:27:07.250
So when you update
the machine, it

00:27:07.250 --> 00:27:10.410
will continue running,
even through the update.

00:27:10.410 --> 00:27:13.040
You'll never have
downtime because we're

00:27:13.040 --> 00:27:14.540
updating the hardware.

00:27:14.540 --> 00:27:16.881
We migrate and
solve that for you,

00:27:16.881 --> 00:27:18.380
so you don't have
to deal with that.

00:27:18.380 --> 00:27:22.610
So in each of these cases, you
get that with whatever solution

00:27:22.610 --> 00:27:23.300
you build.

00:27:23.300 --> 00:27:26.540
But we won't update the
software or run backups for you

00:27:26.540 --> 00:27:30.880
for them if we don't say we
do, like we do with Cloud SQL.

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:33.364
All right, so now, here we are.

00:27:33.364 --> 00:27:34.280
We have taken our app.

00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:35.150
We've moved to the cloud.

00:27:35.150 --> 00:27:36.140
We've put it on a VM.

00:27:36.140 --> 00:27:39.320
And then we've offloaded
database and storage

00:27:39.320 --> 00:27:41.370
to different parts of the cloud.

00:27:41.370 --> 00:27:43.280
So now, I'm free
to start thinking,

00:27:43.280 --> 00:27:46.210
like, well, since
all of the data,

00:27:46.210 --> 00:27:49.179
of the state of this
application is elsewhere,

00:27:49.179 --> 00:27:51.470
then I can start making
interesting decisions about how

00:27:51.470 --> 00:27:53.060
I run this.

00:27:53.060 --> 00:27:56.480
Do I want to run a group of
VMs behind a load balancer

00:27:56.480 --> 00:27:59.900
to make sure that I've
always got redundancy?

00:27:59.900 --> 00:28:01.020
I can do that.

00:28:01.020 --> 00:28:03.480
If I want to go to a serverless
solution, I can do that.

00:28:03.480 --> 00:28:05.540
If I want to go to
Kubernetes, I can do that.

00:28:05.540 --> 00:28:09.560
And I'll talk a little bit
about how to do this with a VM.

00:28:09.560 --> 00:28:12.170
So how do I scale the system?

00:28:12.170 --> 00:28:16.770
So I'm going to switch to demo
one last thing, I believe.

00:28:16.770 --> 00:28:20.330
And that's going to be something
we call managed instance

00:28:20.330 --> 00:28:21.720
groups.

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:26.040
So another error, just refresh.

00:28:26.040 --> 00:28:26.910
And there we go.

00:28:26.910 --> 00:28:31.870
So these take minutes to get
rolling instead of seconds.

00:28:31.870 --> 00:28:34.710
So I'm not, again, not going
to strain our relationship

00:28:34.710 --> 00:28:37.170
and do things that take
that long in front of you.

00:28:37.170 --> 00:28:40.020
But I will kind of walk you
through creating a managed

00:28:40.020 --> 00:28:40.680
instance group.

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:44.680
So basically, I define a group.

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:48.160
I say, I want this to be a
group of tagger machines.

00:28:48.160 --> 00:28:49.970
I want them all to
have tagger on it.

00:28:49.970 --> 00:28:52.480
I want them all to run
with the same software.

00:28:52.480 --> 00:28:54.820
And so what I do
is, I could say--

00:28:54.820 --> 00:28:57.790
well, I could put this in
a single location or multi.

00:28:57.790 --> 00:29:00.540
So I can have these groups
all around the world.

00:29:00.540 --> 00:29:01.790
I put it in a particular zone.

00:29:01.790 --> 00:29:03.760
And then I can be
managed or unmanaged.

00:29:03.760 --> 00:29:06.560
Managed means that every
machine is based on a template.

00:29:06.560 --> 00:29:08.360
And they look exactly the same.

00:29:08.360 --> 00:29:11.480
So we can do things
like, say, autoscale.

00:29:11.480 --> 00:29:14.360
So all the machines
are exactly the same.

00:29:14.360 --> 00:29:18.370
If the CPU utilization ever gets
above 60, spin up another one,

00:29:18.370 --> 00:29:22.420
and then spin up another one,
until it gets down below 60,

00:29:22.420 --> 00:29:23.420
and it gets scaled down.

00:29:23.420 --> 00:29:24.836
And so I can say
things like, make

00:29:24.836 --> 00:29:27.790
sure there are always
three, no more than 10,

00:29:27.790 --> 00:29:30.500
and scale with 60%
processor utilization,

00:29:30.500 --> 00:29:34.320
which is what I have for
the one that I have there.

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:35.500
And so I can build that.

00:29:35.500 --> 00:29:39.549
And you'll see that I did
here in this interface.

00:29:39.549 --> 00:29:40.840
So I have these three machines.

00:29:40.840 --> 00:29:42.640
And if I click
through, I can see

00:29:42.640 --> 00:29:44.950
that each one of these
machines is running.

00:29:44.950 --> 00:29:47.500
Each one of these machines
is open to the internet.

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:50.080
And each one of these
machines is running Apache,

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:51.370
just like I want it to.

00:29:51.370 --> 00:29:53.190
So we're all good.

00:29:53.190 --> 00:29:56.580
So where'd I go?

00:29:56.580 --> 00:29:57.990
So the next thing
I want to do is

00:29:57.990 --> 00:30:00.930
take this and make it
redundant, basically share it

00:30:00.930 --> 00:30:01.776
with the world.

00:30:01.776 --> 00:30:03.900
To share it with the world,
I need a load balancer.

00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:06.967
So I'm going to pull in
tagger load balancer, which

00:30:06.967 --> 00:30:07.800
I've already set up.

00:30:07.800 --> 00:30:09.210
And again, this takes
a little while to go.

00:30:09.210 --> 00:30:11.470
So I'm just going to walk you
through the way it's set up.

00:30:11.470 --> 00:30:12.261
It's pretty simple.

00:30:12.261 --> 00:30:13.470
I say, give me a front end.

00:30:13.470 --> 00:30:14.700
It's HTTP.

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:18.090
It's on port 80 and serve
it up an IP address.

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:23.160
And then I say, from the back
end, serve this instance group.

00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:25.890
So this instance group,
everything is open on port 80.

00:30:25.890 --> 00:30:27.500
You're listening on port 80.

00:30:27.500 --> 00:30:29.970
Combine them and
make that redundant.

00:30:29.970 --> 00:30:32.536
So once I do that, I have
the setup the way it is.

00:30:32.536 --> 00:30:34.410
Each one of those machines
is answering calls

00:30:34.410 --> 00:30:35.220
to the internet.

00:30:35.220 --> 00:30:37.770
But if I go to this
machine, this IP address

00:30:37.770 --> 00:30:43.769
that I get here, it's
also up and running.

00:30:43.769 --> 00:30:45.310
And so like I said,
these will scale.

00:30:45.310 --> 00:30:47.500
All of that scaling
can happen, usually,

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:49.349
within a minute to two minutes.

00:30:49.349 --> 00:30:50.890
So if you start
getting a load spike,

00:30:50.890 --> 00:30:55.450
we can scale up load relatively
quickly, but not necessarily

00:30:55.450 --> 00:30:57.760
instantaneously, OK?

00:30:57.760 --> 00:31:00.580
So that is scaling
out this application

00:31:00.580 --> 00:31:02.820
with a load balancer.

00:31:02.820 --> 00:31:07.741
So with that, I'm going to
switch back to presenting

00:31:07.741 --> 00:31:09.490
and take you through
a couple more things.

00:31:09.490 --> 00:31:12.696
So we talked about instance
groups, which are--

00:31:12.696 --> 00:31:14.070
they can be managed
or unmanaged.

00:31:14.070 --> 00:31:15.390
Why would they be unmanaged?

00:31:15.390 --> 00:31:19.614
Well, maybe you want to combine
a two-CPU box and a 60-CPU box.

00:31:19.614 --> 00:31:21.030
I don't know why,
but you want to.

00:31:21.030 --> 00:31:23.520
That's cool-- no judgment.

00:31:23.520 --> 00:31:26.040
You're responsible for
scaling and all that,

00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:28.110
because we can't figure
out CPU utilization--

00:31:28.110 --> 00:31:30.060
like, what should we add?

00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:31.460
We don't have any
of that set up.

00:31:31.460 --> 00:31:33.030
We're not using a template.

00:31:33.030 --> 00:31:34.230
So you can manage that.

00:31:34.230 --> 00:31:36.477
But if you use managed,
we can auto scale.

00:31:36.477 --> 00:31:37.560
And we can also auto heal.

00:31:37.560 --> 00:31:41.040
If you have health checks on
your app, and it goes down,

00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:43.770
we can rebuild a VM for
you and bring it back

00:31:43.770 --> 00:31:46.639
into the instance group.

00:31:46.639 --> 00:31:48.430
Networking, we talked
about load balancers.

00:31:48.430 --> 00:31:50.290
We have HTTP and
HTTPS load balancer,

00:31:50.290 --> 00:31:53.350
which is equivalent of an
L7, if you're used to that.

00:31:53.350 --> 00:31:55.296
We also do TCP and
UDP load balancing.

00:31:55.296 --> 00:31:56.920
And we have internal
only that are only

00:31:56.920 --> 00:32:00.980
accessible within your project.

00:32:00.980 --> 00:32:03.800
Now, that's how I scale VMs.

00:32:03.800 --> 00:32:05.724
But I do work for Google Cloud.

00:32:05.724 --> 00:32:07.265
And we use the
buzzwords, "container"

00:32:07.265 --> 00:32:08.819
and "servers," a lot.

00:32:08.819 --> 00:32:11.360
So I felt like it was necessary
to at least talk about these.

00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:14.140
How many people are
already using, Docker?

00:32:14.140 --> 00:32:18.530
OK, how many people
are using Kubernetes?

00:32:18.530 --> 00:32:19.296
OK.

00:32:19.296 --> 00:32:21.170
All right, for those of
you that aren't, I'll

00:32:21.170 --> 00:32:23.100
give just a quick
what is containers

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:24.720
at a very, very high level.

00:32:24.720 --> 00:32:26.360
And watch the faces
of the people that

00:32:26.360 --> 00:32:29.120
raised their hands as
they squirm and scream

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:32.270
through this really, really
oversimplified definition.

00:32:32.270 --> 00:32:34.587
Containers are
basically a package

00:32:34.587 --> 00:32:36.170
that contains all
the code and runtime

00:32:36.170 --> 00:32:39.290
components to run a
particular process, your app.

00:32:39.290 --> 00:32:42.500
And then there's an environment,
Docker or Kubernetes,

00:32:42.500 --> 00:32:44.442
that will trick the
process into thinking

00:32:44.442 --> 00:32:46.150
it's the only one
running on the machine.

00:32:46.150 --> 00:32:47.649
And so you can do
crazy things, like

00:32:47.649 --> 00:32:50.030
have multiple versions
of the same software

00:32:50.030 --> 00:32:51.500
running on the same machine.

00:32:51.500 --> 00:32:54.980
You can stack as many more
processes on a machine

00:32:54.980 --> 00:32:57.350
that you can do VMs
on the same hardware.

00:32:57.350 --> 00:33:02.300
So basically, there are a
lot of efficiencies you get

00:33:02.300 --> 00:33:04.490
from switching to this model.

00:33:04.490 --> 00:33:06.920
We have two products
that we say are container

00:33:06.920 --> 00:33:08.594
products on Google Cloud.

00:33:08.594 --> 00:33:10.010
There are Kubernetes
Engine, which

00:33:10.010 --> 00:33:12.635
is managed Kubernetes, which, if
you don't know what Kubernetes

00:33:12.635 --> 00:33:16.130
is, it's basically, you
have dozens, or hundreds,

00:33:16.130 --> 00:33:18.410
or thousands of containers
all doing different things.

00:33:18.410 --> 00:33:21.050
You want to run them in
a way that you're not

00:33:21.050 --> 00:33:22.797
managing individual containers.

00:33:22.797 --> 00:33:24.380
You put them into a
Kubernetes cluster

00:33:24.380 --> 00:33:26.107
with instructions
on how to run them.

00:33:26.107 --> 00:33:27.940
And it'll keep them
running, whether they're

00:33:27.940 --> 00:33:32.870
a microservice, a web app,
or a batch job somewhere.

00:33:32.870 --> 00:33:35.000
App Engine flexible is, if
you just want something,

00:33:35.000 --> 00:33:37.036
you have one app that
you just want to scale,

00:33:37.036 --> 00:33:38.660
and it's already in
a Docker container,

00:33:38.660 --> 00:33:40.990
you can push it to
App Engine flexible,

00:33:40.990 --> 00:33:43.310
and we will scale
it for you, from one

00:33:43.310 --> 00:33:46.980
to as many instances
of it you need.

00:33:46.980 --> 00:33:48.720
What is serverless?

00:33:48.720 --> 00:33:52.480
So serverless is
a marketing term.

00:33:52.480 --> 00:33:53.470
It is, right?

00:33:53.470 --> 00:33:55.470
Like, there is a server there.

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:58.470
There's not some beast
living beyond space and time

00:33:58.470 --> 00:34:01.350
running your server--
your load for you.

00:34:01.350 --> 00:34:04.790
But it describes, I think, a
really important distinction,

00:34:04.790 --> 00:34:07.320
a different type of app,
which is, you pay as you go.

00:34:07.320 --> 00:34:08.760
Whatever you use, you pay for.

00:34:08.760 --> 00:34:11.040
If you don't use it,
you don't pay for it.

00:34:11.040 --> 00:34:13.630
And you don't care about the
underlying infrastructure.

00:34:13.630 --> 00:34:15.588
You don't have to know
about number of servers,

00:34:15.588 --> 00:34:17.010
or number of nodes, or anything.

00:34:17.010 --> 00:34:21.030
We will just run it for you as
much or as little as you need.

00:34:21.030 --> 00:34:23.789
So when we talk about serverless
on Google Cloud Platform,

00:34:23.789 --> 00:34:25.080
we're talking about two things.

00:34:25.080 --> 00:34:28.380
We're talking Cloud Functions,
which the unit of abstraction

00:34:28.380 --> 00:34:30.396
is a function, right?

00:34:30.396 --> 00:34:32.520
And you attach that function
to some sort of event.

00:34:32.520 --> 00:34:35.670
Someone drops a file
somewhere into a bucket.

00:34:35.670 --> 00:34:37.830
Someone sends a
message Pub/Sub queue,

00:34:37.830 --> 00:34:40.949
you respond to it with
that simple Cloud Function.

00:34:40.949 --> 00:34:43.770
We also have a serverless
solution that's app based.

00:34:43.770 --> 00:34:46.812
Now, it's very restricted
in terms of what you can do.

00:34:46.812 --> 00:34:48.270
You can only use
certain languages.

00:34:48.270 --> 00:34:49.920
You can't use third-party code.

00:34:49.920 --> 00:34:51.780
You can only use code
in that language.

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:53.880
But what you get
for that restriction

00:34:53.880 --> 00:34:56.280
is incredible scaling.

00:34:56.280 --> 00:34:59.460
App Engine can scale from
zero to pretty much infinite

00:34:59.460 --> 00:35:01.140
in a relatively
short period of time.

00:35:01.140 --> 00:35:05.280
I have a demo where I
will scale an app that's

00:35:05.280 --> 00:35:09.090
cold, like I didn't use
it, to about 8,500 QPS--

00:35:09.090 --> 00:35:13.440
Queries Per Second--
in about five minutes.

00:35:13.440 --> 00:35:15.570
And to put that in
perspective, 8,500 QPS

00:35:15.570 --> 00:35:17.550
is what Wikipedia gets.

00:35:17.550 --> 00:35:19.710
So I'm able to go
from nothing, cold,

00:35:19.710 --> 00:35:23.280
to a Wikipedia-scale application
in five minutes with App

00:35:23.280 --> 00:35:23.820
Engine.

00:35:23.820 --> 00:35:25.500
But again, it comes at a cost--

00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:28.210
restricted abilities
to do things.

00:35:28.210 --> 00:35:30.630
So the question is, should I
switch over to one of these?

00:35:30.630 --> 00:35:32.160
That's like a whole talk--

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:33.990
that's a whole separate
talk, of whether I

00:35:33.990 --> 00:35:36.720
should use serverless, virtual
machine, or containers.

00:35:36.720 --> 00:35:41.310
But I'll say, for this app,
without any context, no.

00:35:41.310 --> 00:35:42.670
It's working where it is.

00:35:42.670 --> 00:35:44.370
I'm bringing it over
to someplace else.

00:35:44.370 --> 00:35:46.870
I don't necessarily want to
put a lot of effort into it.

00:35:46.870 --> 00:35:48.930
Lift and shift is
absolutely fine.

00:35:48.930 --> 00:35:52.710
If I have plans to
grow this app bigger,

00:35:52.710 --> 00:35:55.230
or to grow it with a whole
bunch of other applications,

00:35:55.230 --> 00:35:57.180
maybe, it might make
sense to containerize.

00:35:57.180 --> 00:35:59.050
But again, that would
depend on the context.

00:35:59.050 --> 00:36:01.470
And if I'm going to have
very inconsistent use,

00:36:01.470 --> 00:36:04.440
then maybe serverless
would work out, too.

00:36:04.440 --> 00:36:06.540
But for this particular
app, with no context,

00:36:06.540 --> 00:36:07.623
I'm not switching it over.

00:36:07.623 --> 00:36:10.020
I'm staying on VMs.

00:36:10.020 --> 00:36:11.430
OK, we've come to the end.

00:36:11.430 --> 00:36:14.370
I've taken you through a pretty
good tour of what we have here.

00:36:14.370 --> 00:36:15.660
We started here.

00:36:15.660 --> 00:36:18.660
We started with a single app,
all running on one machine.

00:36:18.660 --> 00:36:20.852
But we ended up here,
where we've basically

00:36:20.852 --> 00:36:22.560
taken all the state,
pushed it elsewhere.

00:36:22.560 --> 00:36:24.510
And we can now grow
and expand, have

00:36:24.510 --> 00:36:27.730
a lot of different options
with the computing level.

00:36:27.730 --> 00:36:30.000
Now, there was so
